Honour -Dx11=disabled instead of guessing X11 from the headers.

suil_config.h defines HAVE_X11 from __has_include(<X11/Xlib.h>) unless the build
system defines SUIL_NO_DEFAULT_CONFIG, and meson never defines either. The guess
is wrong on Windows: mingw-w64's tk package installs X11 headers, so HAVE_X11
gets defined on a system with no X server, and suil_init() then tries to dlopen
a suil_x11 module that -Dx11=disabled never built. The result is an error on
stderr from every host that calls suil_init().

Define HAVE_X11 explicitly when the x11 dependency was actually found, and
suppress the header's autodetection otherwise. X11 is the only feature the
default-config block gates, so disabling it costs nothing else.

Upstream: not submitted
Applies-to: all environments

diff --git a/meson.build b/meson.build
index be54345..5d16cbd 100644
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -226,6 +226,16 @@ x11_dep = dependency(
   required: get_option('x11'),
 )
 
+# Tell suil_config.h whether X11 is really available instead of letting it guess
+# from __has_include(<X11/Xlib.h>).  The headers can be installed on platforms
+# that have no X server and where no suil_x11 module was built, in which case
+# the guess is wrong and suil_init() fails to load a module that does not exist.
+if x11_dep.found()
+  platform_defines += ['-DHAVE_X11']
+else
+  platform_defines += ['-DSUIL_NO_DEFAULT_CONFIG']
+endif
+
 gtk2_dep = dependency(
   'gtk+-2.0',
   include_type: 'system',
